//OUT="0 done"

// A negative level cannot be written at all. The tokenizer emits `-` as its own
// symbol and never folds it into the literal that follows, so `brk -1` is not
// `brk` carrying a level of -1 — the level slot sees no integer, and the line
// parses as a bare `brk` followed by `-1` as an expression statement
// (03_operators/27_an_expression_statement_is_allowed.cpl), which the `brk` has
// already jumped past. The loop leaves after one pass, exactly as
// 29_break_one_is_the_default_level.cpl does. `cnt -1` splits the same way.
//
// So zero is the only invalid level the parser can ever see, and 63e/64e are
// the whole of that story — there is no negative-level diagnostic to test.
//
// 65-67 are numbered after the error block: they belong beside the level cases
// at 29-33, but renumbering 50e-64e to make room is not worth the churn.

fn main
    for i 0..3
        out "{i} "
        brk -1
    out "done"
